-- title:
--   Semistable specialisation with supersingular nodes, toric monodromy and Hecke transport
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a nonzero natural number and $q'$ a prime not dividing $M$, and let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $q'$ in its set of nonunits, whose residue field $\kappa$ has decidable equality and for which the set of supersingular places $\mathrm{ssPlaces}\ q'\ M\ \kappa$ of the level-$M$ function field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ \kappa\ M$ is finite; the degree-zero divisor class groups $\mathrm{JZero}\,N$ carry the Hecke-algebra structures $\mathrm{heckeModuleBar}$, where $\mathrm{HeckeAlg}=\mathbb{Z}[X_\ell:\ell\text{ prime}]$. Then there exist a $\mathrm{HeckeAlg}$-module structure on $\mathrm{Pic0}\ \kappa\ (\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ \kappa\ M)$ and a term $D$ of `JZeroSemistableSpecialization A M q' hq'` — the structure packaging a finite set $D.\mathrm{nodes}$ of pairs of $\kappa$-rational places of the level-$M$ function field, a semilinear automorphism $D.\mathrm{frob}$ acting on $\kappa$ by $a\mapsto a^{q'}$ and stabilising the nodes with involutive induced permutation, node widths, a component map $D.\mathrm{comp}$ from the inertia invariants $\mathrm{JZero}(Mq')^{I_A}$ to the component group of the widths, and a specialisation homomorphism $D.\mathrm{sp}$ into the glued Picard group $\mathrm{GluedPic0}$, subject to Hecke and Frobenius compatibilities — such that: (i) $D.\mathrm{nodes}$ is exactly the image of the supersingular places under $w\mapsto(w,D.\mathrm{frob}\cdot w)$; (ii) for every $\sigma$ in $A.\mathrm{inertiaSubgroupIn}\ \mathbb{Q}$ and every $x\in\mathrm{JZero}(Mq')$ killed by some positive integer prime to $q'$, the difference $\sigma\cdot x-x$ is inertia-invariant, is annihilated by $D.\mathrm{comp}$, and its specialisation lies in the kernel of $\mathrm{toPic0Pair}$; (iii) conversely every $z$ with $\mathrm{toPic0Pair}\,z=0$ equals $D.\mathrm{sp}\,y$ for some inertia-invariant $y$ in the subgroup of $\mathrm{JZero}(Mq')$ generated by all such differences $\sigma\cdot x-x$; (iv) for each prime $\ell$ there are an integer matrix $T_\ell$ indexed by the nodes and an integer $n_\ell$ with $\sum_t (T_\ell)_{t s}=n_\ell$ for all $s$, such that whenever $x$ and $X_\ell\cdot x$ are inertia-invariant, $D.\mathrm{comp}\,x=0$ and $D.\mathrm{sp}\,x=\mathrm{nodeUnit}\,w$ for some $w:D.\mathrm{nodes}\to\mathrm{Additive}\ \kappa^\times$, one has $D.\mathrm{sp}(X_\ell\cdot x)=\mathrm{nodeUnit}\bigl(t\mapsto\sum_s (T_\ell)_{s t}\cdot w_s\bigr)$; and (v) $D.\mathrm{comp}$ is surjective.
--
--   This is the form in which the toric (monodromy) part of the special fibre of $J_0(Mq')$ at a place over $q'$ is made available: the character group of the torus is swept out by inertial differences $\sigma x-x$ of torsion points of order prime to $q'$, with nodes indexed by supersingular places and an integral transport rule for the Hecke operators. It feeds the counting result [`ModularCurve.natCard_closure_inertia_smul_sub_torsionBy_eq_pow_card_ssPlaces`](thm.html#ModularCurve.natCard_closure_inertia_smul_sub_torsionBy_eq_pow_card_ssPlaces), a step in the level-lowering argument at $q'$.
